const a="tirana-to-ksamil-saranda-by-car",t="Tirana to Ksamil and Saranda by Car: Routes, Driving Times and Stops",e="Tirana to Ksamil by Car: Routes, Times & Stops",o="Driving from Tirana to Ksamil or Saranda? Compare inland and coastal routes, driving times, best stops and summer parking tips in Ksamil. Plan ahead.",r="The two ways to drive from Tirana to Saranda and Ksamil, inland via Gjirokastër or along the coast over the Llogara Pass, with driving times, stops and parking tips.",i=["Tirana to Ksamil by car","Tirana to Saranda drive","Tirana to Saranda distance","how long is the drive from Tirana to Ksamil","Llogara Pass","parking in Ksamil"],l=5,n=`<p>The drive from Tirana to Saranda is roughly 280 km and takes about 4.5 to 5 hours on the faster inland route via Tepelenë and Gjirokastër, or around 5.5 to 6.5 hours on the coastal route via Vlorë and the Llogara Pass. Ksamil is a further 15 km south of Saranda, about 20 to 30 minutes away. Below we compare both routes, suggest stops worth making and explain how to deal with parking in Ksamil during the summer.</p><h2>Tirana to Saranda and Ksamil: the two main routes</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Route</th><th>Approx. distance to Saranda</th><th>Approx. driving time (no stops)</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Inland via Fier, Tepelenë and Gjirokastër</td><td>About 280–300 km</td><td>4.5–5 hours</td><td>Speed, easier driving, Blue Eye and Gjirokastër stops</td></tr><tr><td>Coastal via Vlorë, Llogara Pass and Himarë</td><td>About 270–290 km</td><td>5.5–6.5 hours</td><td>Scenery, beaches, a relaxed two-day drive</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Times are approximate and depend on traffic, roadworks and the season. In July and August, add extra time for queues through towns and on the final stretch into Saranda and Ksamil.</p><h2>Route 1: Inland via Gjirokastër (the faster option)</h2><p>From Tirana or the airport, take the main road south towards Durrës and Fier. After Fier, the road continues inland through Levan and along the Vjosa river valley to Tepelenë, then up the Drino valley to Gjirokastër. From there, the road crosses a mountain pass to the Blue Eye and descends to Saranda.</p><p>This route is mostly on good, wide roads, with the winding sections limited to the pass between Gjirokastër and the coast. It is the better choice if you want to reach the beach on the same day, if you are travelling with children, or if you prefer less demanding driving.</p><h3>Stops worth making on the inland route</h3><ul><li><strong>Gjirokastër:</strong> the stone-roofed UNESCO-listed old town and its hilltop castle are worth at least two hours, or an overnight stay.</li><li><strong>The Blue Eye (Syri i Kaltër):</strong> a strikingly blue natural spring a short walk from the car park, just off the road between Gjirokastër and Saranda.</li><li><strong>Tepelenë and the Vjosa valley:</strong> a good place for a coffee break with river views.</li></ul><h2>Route 2: Along the coast via Vlorë and the Llogara Pass (the scenic option)</h2><p>This route follows the main road south to Vlorë, then continues along the bay past Orikum before climbing into the mountains to the Llogara Pass. From the top, the road drops in a series of hairpins with spectacular views over the Ionian Sea, then winds along the Albanian Riviera through Dhërmi, Himarë, Porto Palermo, Borsh and Lukovë before reaching Saranda.</p><p>The road is paved throughout but narrow and winding in places, with slow traffic in summer. It is the most beautiful drive in Albania, but it is tiring to do in one go. Many travellers split it over two days, stopping overnight in Dhërmi or Himarë. A road tunnel has been under construction beneath the Llogara Pass; if it is open when you travel, it can shorten the trip, but the pass road itself is the scenic choice.</p><h3>Stops worth making on the coastal route</h3><ul><li><strong>Vlorë:</strong> the waterfront promenade and a good spot for lunch.</li><li><strong>Llogara Pass:</strong> pine forest, cool air and a viewpoint at the top over the Riviera.</li><li><strong>Dhërmi and Jalë:</strong> beautiful beaches just below the main road.</li><li><strong>Himarë:</strong> a relaxed town with Livadh beach nearby.</li><li><strong>Porto Palermo:</strong> a small fortress on a peninsula, right next to the road.</li><li><strong>Borsh:</strong> one of the longest beaches on the Riviera.</li></ul><p>For details on each beach, see our <a href="/blog/albanian-riviera-beaches-by-car">guide to Albanian Riviera beaches by car</a>.</p><h2>Which route should you choose?</h2><ul><li><strong>You want to get there fast:</strong> take the inland route.</li><li><strong>You have time and want the views:</strong> take the coastal route and break the journey.</li><li><strong>The best of both:</strong> drive down inland via Gjirokastër and the Blue Eye, then return north along the coast. This loop is the backbone of our <a href="/blog/albania-road-trip-itinerary">Albania road trip itinerary</a>.</li></ul><h2>Saranda to Ksamil</h2><p>From Saranda, Ksamil is about 15 km south on the road to Butrint. The drive normally takes 20 to 30 minutes, but in the peak weeks of summer traffic can be slow, particularly in the late morning and early evening. Butrint National Park lies a few kilometres beyond Ksamil and is easy to combine with a beach day.</p><h2>Parking in Ksamil in summer</h2><p>Ksamil is small and was not designed for the number of cars it receives in July and August. A few tips:</p><ul><li><strong>Book accommodation with parking.</strong> This is the single most useful thing you can do. Confirm it with your host before you arrive.</li><li><strong>Use paid car parks.</strong> Several private parking lots operate near the beaches in summer. Carry cash in lek.</li><li><strong>Arrive early.</strong> Before about 10:00 it is much easier to find space near the beach.</li><li><strong>Park once, then walk.</strong> Most of Ksamil's beaches are within walking distance of each other.</li><li><strong>Do not block driveways or narrow lanes</strong>, and avoid leaving valuables in the car.</li></ul><p>A compact car is much easier to park in Ksamil than a large SUV. If you are travelling as a couple, an economy model is the practical choice for the south.</p><h2>Practical tips for the drive south</h2><ul><li><strong>Start early</strong> to avoid the midday heat and arrive in daylight.</li><li><strong>Fill up before the mountain sections.</strong> Fuel stations are common on the main road, but less so on the pass roads.</li><li><strong>Use the air conditioning</strong> and carry water. Summer temperatures in the south are high.</li><li><strong>Allow for slow traffic</strong> behind trucks on the hills, and only overtake where you can see clearly.</li><li><strong>Read up on local driving habits</strong> in our <a href="/blog/driving-in-albania-guide">driving in Albania guide</a>.</li></ul><h2>Driving there with a rental car from Tirana</h2><p>Picking up a car at Tirana airport and driving south is usually more flexible than taking the bus, which does not stop at the Blue Eye, Llogara or the smaller beaches. With Rent Ride you can collect the car with free meet &amp; greet at the airport or have it delivered free to central Tirana, with 24/7 support if you need help along the way. Cancellation is free up to 48 hours before pickup, and no security deposit is required. <a href="/makine-me-qira-aeroport">Book your car at Tirana airport</a> and start your journey south.</p>`,s=[{question:"How long is the drive from Tirana to Ksamil?",answer:"Allow about 5 to 5.5 hours of driving from Tirana to Ksamil on the inland route via Gjirokastër, without stops. The coastal route via the Llogara Pass usually takes 6 to 7 hours. In July and August, traffic around Saranda and Ksamil can add extra time, so plan an early start."},{question:"What is the distance from Tirana to Saranda by car?",answer:"The distance from Tirana to Saranda is roughly 280 km, whether you take the inland route via Tepelenë and Gjirokastër or the coastal route via Vlorë and the Llogara Pass. The coastal road is not much longer, but it is far more winding, which makes it noticeably slower."},{question:"Is the Llogara Pass difficult to drive?",answer:"The Llogara Pass is paved and used by buses and trucks every day, but it has many hairpin bends and steep sections. Drive slowly, use a lower gear on the descent to protect the brakes, and avoid driving it after dark. Confident drivers find it one of the highlights of a trip to Albania."},{question:"Is it easy to find parking in Ksamil?",answer:"Outside the peak summer weeks, parking in Ksamil is usually easy. In July and August it is very busy, so book accommodation with its own parking, use paid car parks near the beaches and arrive before about 10:00. Once parked, walking between beaches is easier than driving."}],h={slug:a,title:t,metaTitle:e,metaDescription:o,excerpt:r,keywords:i,readingMinutes:5,html:n,faq:s};export{h as default,r as excerpt,s as faq,n as html,i as keywords,o as metaDescription,e as metaTitle,l as readingMinutes,a as slug,t as title};
